URGENT, TIME-CRITICAL. GOAL: fix the injected bug in `objToLocalTS` in /luxon/src/impl/util.js so that `DateTime#plus works across the 100 barrier when passing through February` passes.

Current code (read the file to confirm, ~line 157):

```js
export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
  let d = Date.UTC(
    obj.year,
    obj.month - 1,
    obj.day,
    obj.hour,
    obj.minute,
    obj.second,
    obj.millisecond
  );

  // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
  if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
    d = new Date(d);
    d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
  }
  return +d;
}
```

BUG: `Date.UTC(99, ...)` is interpreted as year 1999, and shifting the year by -1900 afterwards keeps the month/day of a leap-year-2000 calendar, so a date that lands on Feb 29 of 2000 (e.g. `DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ days: 59 })` or `.plus({ months: 2 })` after day overflow) becomes Mar 1 of year 100 instead of Feb 28 of year 100 (year 100 is NOT a leap year).

REQUIRED FIX — replace the body of the `if` with a single `setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day)` call, i.e. the function must become exactly:

```js
export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
  let d = Date.UTC(
    obj.year,
    obj.month - 1,
    obj.day,
    obj.hour,
    obj.minute,
    obj.second,
    obj.millisecond
  );

  // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
  if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
    d = new Date(d);
    // set the month and day again, this is necessary because year 2000 is a leap year, but year 100 is not
    d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
  }
  return +d;
}
```

